---
import GuideLayout from '@layouts/GuideLayout.astro';
import ReferenceBlock from '@components/ReferenceBlock.astro';
import { config } from './config';
import { toolPath } from '@lib/paths';
import { toolPathBySlug } from '@lib/tools/tool-link';
import { categories } from '@data/categories';
import type { EcosystemEntry } from '@data/types';

interface Props {
  title: string;
  description: string;
  readMinutes: number;
  updatedAt: string;
  entry: EcosystemEntry;
}

const props = Astro.props;
const category = categories.find(c => c.slug === config.categorySlug)!;
const toolHref = toolPath({ slug: config.slug, segment: category.segment });
const zoneHref = toolPathBySlug('timezone-converter');
const unixHref = toolPathBySlug('unix-timestamp-converter');
const diffHref = toolPathBySlug('date-difference-calculator');
---

<GuideLayout
  {...props}
  toolSlug={config.slug}
  toolName={config.name}
  toolCategorySlug={config.categorySlug}
>
  <section id="quick-answer">
    <h2>Is 03/04/2026 April or March? <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      Both, until a locale names the order. Day first, 03/04/2026 is 3 April 2026, and month
      first it is 4 March 2026. An ambiguous date format is a row that is day first or month first.
      For example, 11/12/2026 is 11 December or 12 November.
    </p>
    <p>
      13/04/2026 has one reading, 13 April, because month 13 does not exist. 04/13/2026 is the
      same day, read month first. A 13 settles that one line. The rows that can be read as two
      different days are the ones where both numbers are 12 or under.
    </p>
    <p>
      Check a pasted column for dates that can be read two ways when someone asks is this date dd/mm or mm/dd. This page is a date format checker, not an
      ambiguous date parser, and it leaves DD/MM or MM/DD unchosen. The check works by reading
      one date per line. Nothing is uploaded, and the paste is not rewritten.
    </p>
    <a href={toolHref} class="cta-link">Check a column of dates →</a>
  </section>

  <section id="column">
    <h2>Which rows in a column actually swap? <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      A scan for a day above 12 finds 13/04/2026 and then stops. It misses 03/04/2026. In a
      European export opened under a US locale, the rows that move are the ones with both
      numbers at 12 or under. The row with a 13 in it was never at risk.
    </p>
    <p>
      For example, paste 03/04/2026, then 2026-04-13, then April 13, 2026. The count is 1 of 3.
      The note says that one row can be read as two different days. When every row has one
      reading, that note stays hidden, so a clean column does not look like a warning.
    </p>
    <p>
      A column is usually written one way. When a line such as 13/04/2026 only works day first,
      and no line only works month first, the hero says Day first and cites that line. The
      ambiguous rows are then read day first, and each one names the line that decided it. Month
      first works the same way. When one line only works day first and another only works month
      first, the hero says Mixed, cites one line for each, and leaves the rows ambiguous. ISO and
      named-month dates never decide the order.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>Two readings, when both numbers are 12 or under and the two orders are different days.</li>
      <li>One reading, when one number is above 12 and that day exists, or a named month or a year-first ISO date is a real day. 08/08/2026 is one day too: both orders are 8 August, so it is not counted.</li>
      <li>Missing zone, when a time has no offset.</li>
      <li>Two-digit year, when the year is only two digits and the day exists.</li>
      <li>Unreadable, when the line is not a date or the day is not on the calendar, such as 31/04/2026 or 2026-04-31. The line is quoted, and it is not also counted as a missing zone or a two-digit year.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Open How this was worked out for the line list. The first 20 rows are written out, and a
      two-reading row there shows the raw text and both civil dates. A later two-reading row stays
      in the count and is not written out. The paste stops at 200 lines, so line 201 onward is not read.
    </p>
    <ReferenceBlock type="common-mistake" heading="Assuming one deciding line settles every row">
      <p>
        A 13 proves its own row. It settles 03/04/2026 only if the whole column came from one
        source. A merged export can hold both orders, which is why the tool says Mixed as soon as
        a line only works the other way. Check for Mixed before you trust a verdict.
      </p>
    </ReferenceBlock>
  </section>

  <section id="year">
    <h2>How is a two-digit year pivoted? <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      00 to 69 are read as 2000 to 2069, and 70 to 99 are read as 1970 to 1999. For example,
      03/04/26 is still two readings in 2026: 3 April 2026 or 4 March 2026. 03/04/70 is 1970.
      The row is marked Two-digit year because this pivot is a choice, not a fact in the file.
    </p>
    <p>
      Assuming a two-digit year always means 20xx is the mistake. A source system splits the
      century somewhere else. The assumption sits with the result so you can see the years this
      page used. It will not ask you to pick a window.
    </p>
    <ReferenceBlock type="common-mistake" heading="Assuming a two-digit year always means 20xx">
      <p>
        69 maps to 2069. 70 maps to 1970. For example, 03/04/70 is 3 April 1970 or 4 March 1970.
        A four-digit year stays as written, so 2026 stays 2026.
      </p>
    </ReferenceBlock>
  </section>

  <section id="zone">
    <h2>When can a timestamp land a day early? <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      2026-04-03T18:00:00 has a time and no offset. The civil date stays 3 April 2026, and the
      row is counted under Missing zone. Apply a zone later and 18:00 with no offset can fall
      on 2 April or on 4 April. 2026-04-03T18:00:00Z already names UTC, so it is not flagged.
    </p>
    <p>
      2026-04-03 has no time, so a date alone is not a zoneless timestamp. The page does not
      invent a zone to clear the flag. Once you know the instant, the
      <a href={zoneHref}>timezone converter</a> applies one. A pinned date can go to the
      <a href={unixHref}>Unix timestamp converter</a> when the next step is an epoch number.
    </p>
    <ReferenceBlock type="common-mistake" heading="Treating a bare timestamp as a stored day">
      <p>
        A bare timestamp is not a finished day. For example, 2026-04-03T18:00:00 can move to
        2 April once a zone is applied. Avoid guessing the offset. The warning is the flag.
      </p>
    </ReferenceBlock>
  </section>

  <section id="next">
    <h2>How does an ISO 8601 date differ from a US date? <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      An ISO 8601 date such as 2026-04-03 has one reading, because the year comes first.
      Unlike 03/04/2026, it does not swap month and day. The comparison iso 8601 vs us date
      is that split: the US reading of 03/04/2026 is 4 March, and 2026-04-03 is 3 April.
    </p>
    <p>
      After an export, excel date locale wrong is what you see when an Excel locale assumed
      month first. Ask how will this date be parsed before a spreadsheet stores the column.
      This page lists both readings. It does not decide which one Excel keeps.
    </p>
    <p>
      It does not count the span between two chosen dates, so that job is the
      <a href={diffHref}>date difference calculator</a>. It does not convert a clock from one
      city to another, so that job is the <a href={zoneHref}>timezone converter</a>. Therefore
      the next export either shows a count of rows that swap, or stays quiet.
    </p>
    <a href={toolHref} class="cta-link">Paste a date column →</a>
  </section>
</GuideLayout>

<style>
  .cta-link {
    display: inline-flex;
    margin-top: var(--space-3);
    font-weight: var(--font-weight-medium);
  }
</style>
